// Indexing a slice is bounds-checked against the length the slice carries.
//
// This is the half of slice indexing that a running program cannot show: the
// check only announces itself by aborting, and a scenario that aborted would
// assert nothing and fail its own run. So it is asserted in the generated LLVM
// IR instead, by the named check on this scenario in cases.toml.
//
// The index here is the constant 0, and the slice arrives as a parameter. That
// pairing is the point: the length is read out of the slice value at run time
// ('extractvalue ... 1'), so the optimizer cannot prove 0 is in range and the
// branch to 'llvm.trap' survives optimization. collection-bounds-fixed is the
// same index against a fixed-size array reached by reference, where the length
// is in the type and the whole check folds away.
//
// One function, because the check matches against the whole IR dump and a
// second function indexing anything else would make it ambiguous which one
// emitted what.

fn first(s &[]i32) i32 {
  s[0]
}
